## THE ARMORY (`story.ni:4703-4735`) — the room; Olgan Minor follows.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

create the Armory
  a room
  aka armory shop, armoury, olgan's shop, minor's shop
  south to the Commerce Street, one-way

  The tangy smell of oiled steel is nearly overpowering in this small,
  windowless shop. All of the really interesting weapons — axes, flails, spiked
  gauntlets and the like — are high up on racks on the back wall, well behind
  the high counter. However, there is a display of assorted knives and daggers
  sitting out where you can get a close look at them.

  after the player going
    phrase armory-relief
  end after

create the dagger display
  aka dagger, daggers, knives, knife, display, blades, blade, stilettos, stiletto, stickers, sticker
  scenery
  buy-from-minor
  in the Armory

  Over a dozen gleaming blades are set out in neat little rows: from long,
  wicked stilettos, to demure little stickers you could hide inside an opera
  glove, to gigantic cleavers that would make Holstenoffer next door jealous.

  on the player taking
    refuse minor-finger-off
  end on

  on the player touching
    refuse minor-finger-off
  end on

create the big weapons
  aka weapons, axes, flails, flail, racks, rack, gauntlets, gauntlet, spiked gauntlets, interesting weapons
  scenery, plural
  in the Armory

  The sheer variety of ways to grievously injure someone with a sharp, metal
  instrument leaves you both impressed and vaguely disturbed.

## `story.ni:4730` — the dirty-Jack variant; Pieter's is a later chapter's.

define phrase minor-finger-off
  'Touch one of those and I'll use it to have your finger off, little thief,'
  growls Minor.
end phrase

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## EAST COMMERCE STREET (`story.ni:4822-4950`) — the estates as locked fronts.
##
## The Red Gate `unlock` machinery (the large brass key) is built with its
## chapter; here the gates are locked. The Jacobs refusal is the pre-jail
## variant; the City Park refusal is the pre-sewer one, `stopping` as the
## source has it. `knock` is a story action: the estates answer, anything
## else gets the source's "Nothing happens."; the source's "I don't suppose
## <person> would care for that" is not carried (no kind test in a story
## action).
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

## `knock` is a story dispatch action, so each estate's answer rides a trait
## (an entity `on` clause never fires on the dispatch path).

define trait knock-black-gate
  on the player knocking
    refuse black-gate-servants
  end on
end trait

define trait knock-red-gate
  on the player knocking
    refuse red-gate-no-answer
  end on
end trait

define trait knock-jacobs
  on the player knocking
    refuse jacobs-no-business
  end on
end trait

create the East Commerce Street
  a room
  aka east end, residential neighborhood, east commerce
  west to the Commerce Street
  east to the Lord's Market
  northwest is blocked: black-gate-servants
  north is blocked: red-gate-locked
  northeast is blocked: jacobs-no-business
  south is blocked: park-kids

  The east end of Commerce Street winds through an upper-class residential
  neighborhood before ending up at Lord's Market. Behind spiked, iron fences
  stand the 'city cottages' of Toresal's wealthy merchants and nobility, built
  tall and narrow to make the best use of limited real estate.

  Three stately houses are built close together on the north side of the road:
  Black Gate Estate to the northwest; Red Gate Estate to the north; and the
  Jacobs family mansion to the northeast. The street itself continues east and
  west, and the City Park lies to the south.

  on every turn while the player is in the East Commerce Street and one chance in 5
    phrase jack-paranoia
  end on

create Black Gate Estate
  aka black gate, fossville's house, baron's house, somber building, estate
  scenery, proper
  knock-black-gate
  in the East Commerce Street

  Black Gate Estate is the home of Baron Fossville, the current Lord of Toresal
  since the old Duke's death (though he has yet to be fully confirmed). It is a
  somber building of dark flagstone and slate, although you've heard the Baron
  keeps a beautiful rooftop garden.

  on the player entering
    refuse black-gate-servants
  end on

  on the player opening
    refuse black-gate-servants
  end on

create Red Gate Estate
  aka red gate, front gates, gates, gate, duke's house, empty house, crumbling house
  scenery, proper
  knock-red-gate
  in the East Commerce Street

  This is where the Duke and former Lord of Toresal lived, until his death
  several years ago. Now it stands empty, slowly crumbling. No one has ever
  made an attempt to claim the property, and the Baron seems content to let it
  remain vacant. The front gates are securely locked.

  on the player entering
    refuse red-gate-locked
  end on

  on the player opening
    refuse red-gate-locked
  end on

  on the player unlocking
    refuse red-gate-locked
  end on

  on the player climbing
    refuse fence-too-high
  end on

create the Jacobs Mansion
  aka jacobs family mansion, jacobs mansion, mansion, castle, miniature castle, jacobs house
  scenery
  knock-jacobs
  in the East Commerce Street

  Jacobs the Elder is rumored to be the richest merchant in Toresal. His lavish
  city residence is built to resemble a miniature castle, complete with
  turrets, crenellations, and a heavy portcullis over the front door.

  on the player entering
    refuse jacobs-no-business
  end on

  on the player opening
    refuse jacobs-no-business
  end on

create the crenellations
  aka turrets, turret, crenellation, portcullis, castle architecture
  scenery, plural
  in the East Commerce Street

  All the castle-style architecture on Jacobs' mansion is a bit ostentatious.

create the City Park
  aka park, meadow, woods, wood, open meadow, park wall, park gate
  scenery, proper
  in the East Commerce Street

  The park is a couple of acres of open meadow and sparse woods in the middle
  of the city, set aside by the previous Lord to promote health and relaxation
  among Toresal's citizens. It still sees strollers and the occasional
  picnicker by day, although it's become infamous as a haunt for cutpurses by
  night. The park is surrounded by a high stone wall, with a gate on the north
  side.

  on the player entering
    refuse park-kids
  end on

create the iron fence
  aka fence, fences, spikes, spike, iron spikes, spiked fence
  scenery
  in the East Commerce Street

  The fence is too high to climb, and topped with sharp iron spikes to boot.

  on the player climbing
    refuse fence-too-high
  end on

define phrase black-gate-servants
  The servants would never let someone like you enter unless you had urgent
  business with the Baron, which they'd never believe you had anyway.
end phrase

define phrase red-gate-locked
  The front gates are securely locked.
end phrase

define phrase red-gate-no-answer
  No one answers.
end phrase

define phrase jacobs-no-business
  You don't have any business with Jacobs, and even if you did, you might
  think twice before knocking on his door. Rumor has it that many of his
  dealings are decidedly less than legal.
end phrase

## `story.ni:4917`, the pre-sewer variant, and the talent-in-children ruling
## (change document, "The talent in children"): the orphans see her.

define phrase park-kids, stopping
  You hesitate. Several street kids have congregated in the middle of the park,
  chasing each other and playing at knights and villains in the late afternoon
  warmth. Some of them know you from Maiden House, and know that you're really
  a girl. That could lead to an awkward situation if one of them called you
  out in front of someone who only knows you as a boy. Especially if that
  someone was Bobby.
or
  No, too risky. Better visit the park another day.
end phrase

define phrase fence-too-high
  The fence is too high to climb, and topped with sharp iron spikes to boot.
end phrase

## `story.ni:4940-4950`

define action knocking
  grammar
    knock the target
    knock on the target
  otherwise refuse knock-nothing

define phrase knock-nothing
  Nothing happens.
end phrase

## ===========================================================================
## THE KEEPERS — Germaise, Old Man Holstenoffer, Olgan Minor
##
## Descriptions, possessions and idle texture are Gentry's. Conversations are
## STUBS under the standing rule: each `start conversation` site is the story
## event it already is and emits one TODO naming the quip; each `talk to` gets
## a one-line TODO so the command never falls to "I don't understand".
## Perception flags (change document, "Who sees her"): Germaise and
## Holstenoffer do NOT perceive; Olgan Minor has the talent — his description
## carries the source's talent sentence verbatim on the first look.
## Not carried: the source's kissing refusals (no kissing action), the
## "player is not on the move" guard on idle rolls (the port rolls every turn
## the keeper is here, as Teisha's tent does).
## ===========================================================================

## --- Germaise the Baker (`story.ni:4351-4383`) -----------------------------

create Germaise
  aka germaise the baker, baker, the baker, proprietor, shopkeeper, shop keeper, merchant, man behind the counter, tall thin man
  a person, proper
  pronouns he
  in the Bakery
  carries the mound of dough

  Germaise the Baker is a tall, thin man from the south. He has a thick accent
  and likes to stroke his sparse moustaches with his fingertips while he talks
  — the lower half of his face is almost always covered with a grayish coating
  of flour. He's a shameless flirt, always eyeballing the young ladies who
  come into his store. It's moments like those that make you grateful for your
  disguise.

  on every turn while Germaise is here and one chance in 5
    phrase germaise-idle
  end on

  on the player talking
    refuse germaise-ge1-stub
  end on

create the moustache
  aka moustaches, mustache, mustaches, germaise's moustache, caterpillar, tracks
  scenery
  in the Bakery

  Germaise's moustache looks like a caterpillar with particularly dirty feet
  walked across his upper lip and left tracks, but he sure is proud of it.

  on the player touching
    refuse moustache-leave-alone
  end on

  on the player taking
    refuse moustache-leave-alone
  end on

create the mound of dough
  aka dough, pastry dough, mound, strips

  The mound of dough is twice the size of Germaise's head, and he keeps
  punching it and squeezing it for all he's worth. It looks like a great way to
  work out your frustration.

  on the player taking
    refuse dough-delicate
  end on

  on the player touching
    refuse dough-delicate
  end on

define phrase germaise-idle, randomly
  Germaise works the dough, pounding and pushing it into shape.
or
  Germaise tosses another handful of flour onto the dough.
or
  Germaise cuts the dough into strips, twists the strips into a complicated
  shape, and slides the whole thing into the glowing mouth of the brick oven
  behind him. Then he slaps another mound of dough onto the counter and starts
  the process all over again.
or
  Germaise peers into the oven, checking on the status of his latest batch.
end phrase

define phrase moustache-leave-alone
  Germaise would probably rather you leave his moustache alone.
end phrase

define phrase dough-delicate
  'Eh, keep away from the pastry dough, Jack,' Germaise warns you. 'This is
  delicate business.'
end phrase

